---
/* The dial — cloned from labs.anyflow.agency's "We are good at" section.
   Measurements in docs/research/components/anyflow-ring.spec.md.

   THE MARK DOES NOT TRAVEL TO THE RING. THE RING RISES TO MEET THE MARK.
   That is the finding the whole section turns on, and it is invisible in a
   screenshot: across 13 scroll samples their mark's centre never moves off
   viewport centre while the ring climbs 882 -> 771 -> 660 -> 549 at exactly 1:1
   with the page. They meet, and only then does the ring start to turn.

   Which makes this section almost free here. parallax.js already glides the
   hash to the exact centre of the viewport by the end of the hero and holds it
   there — so the pinned mark this effect needs already exists, already in the
   right place. Nothing in this file draws the mark; it just gives the ring
   somewhere to rise to.

   The approach needs no JS either. The ring sits in normal flow inside a 100vh
   stage, so scrolling raises it toward the fixed mark on its own. dial.js only
   takes over AFTER the meeting, for the rotation and the pulse.

   What is left on the face is deliberately little: the glass, one hairline
   circle, four engraved words and a single marker that follows the pointer.
   Four coloured segments and a 24-tick chapter ring were there and are not any
   more — the words already name the four disciplines, so the segments were
   decorating a point already made, and the ticks were instrument-cosplay on an
   object that reads as a dial without them. */

/* --- dial geometry -------------------------------------------------------
   Everything is generated from one radius so the shapes stay correct if it
   changes. Angles run from twelve o'clock, clockwise, which is how a dial is
   read — SVG's own 0 is at three o'clock, hence the -cos/+sin pairing. */
const R = 345, C = 350;
const pt = (deg, r = R) => {
  const a = (deg * Math.PI) / 180;
  return [(C + r * Math.sin(a)).toFixed(2), (C - r * Math.cos(a)).toFixed(2)];
};
/* the segment's outer arc — the coloured edge */
const arc = (f, t) => {
  const [x0, y0] = pt(f), [x1, y1] = pt(t);
  return `M ${x0} ${y0} A ${R} ${R} 0 ${t - f > 180 ? 1 : 0} 1 ${x1} ${y1}`;
};
/* the filled wedge under the marker */
const wedge = (f, t) => {
  const [x0, y0] = pt(f), [x1, y1] = pt(t);
  return `M ${C} ${C} L ${x0} ${y0} A ${R} ${R} 0 ${t - f > 180 ? 1 : 0} 1 ${x1} ${y1} Z`;
};

/* The marker: one 45-degree arc, the eighth of the circle the reference gives
   each of its segments. It is the only thing on the face that moves, so it is
   the only thing left on it. */
const MARKER = { from: 22.5, to: 67.5 };

/* An arc for a word to sit on.

   ALL FOUR RUN THE SAME WAY, and that is the point of them.

   The direction of the path decides which way a word faces on the rim: a
   clockwise arc puts the letter tops OUTWARD, a counter-clockwise one puts them
   INWARD. This used to mix the two — clockwise across the top, counter across
   the bottom — because that keeps every word upright to the reader wherever it
   sits. It also means the ring carries two words facing in and two facing out,
   and once the dial rotates that is the thing you notice: four labels that
   cannot agree which way is up.

   Facing them all inward costs the top two their uprightness and buys the
   object its consistency. That is the right trade here, because this is
   engraving on a bezel rather than a caption — it belongs to the ring, not to
   the page, and a real engraved ring reads round.

   r=302 sits inside the rim — the viewBox is 700 and the circle is already
   r=345, so there is no room beyond it — and well clear of the hash, which
   occupies about 182 units. */
const textArc = (bearing, span, r, cw) => {
  const f = cw ? bearing - span : bearing + span;
  const t = cw ? bearing + span : bearing - span;
  const [x0, y0] = pt(f, r), [x1, y1] = pt(t, r);
  return `M ${x0} ${y0} A ${r} ${r} 0 0 ${cw ? 1 : 0} ${x1} ${y1}`;
};
const WORD_R = 302, WORD_SPAN = 40;

const LABELS = [
  /* bearing from twelve, clockwise. cw is false on all four — see textArc. */
  { t: 'Research',    at:   0, cw: false },
  { t: 'Interface',   at:  90, cw: false },
  { t: 'Systems',     at: 180, cw: false },
  { t: 'Prototyping', at: 270, cw: false },
];
---
<section class="dial" data-dial>
  <div class="dial-stage">
    <!-- the two hairlines their stage is ruled with -->
    <span class="dial-rule dial-rule--h" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="dial-rule dial-rule--v" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    <!-- The dial, cloned from thewatch.60fps.fr's HERO (not its loader — the
         loader is a separate 700-box svg with a .progress-arc, and it is the
         wrong object: it never has a size at any scroll position on the page).

         The hero dial is TWO overlaid svgs, and every value here is measured:

         .circle-svg   a faint outer circle at stroke-opacity .1, then FOUR
                       segment groups. Each carries a wedge fill at
                       fill-opacity .07 (painted only for the selected one), two
                       bounding radii at stroke-opacity .1, and an arc at
                       stroke-width 3 in its OWN colour. Those four colours are
                       the watch variants, and they are why the reference shows a
                       black arc up top and a pale cream one below at the same
                       time. Their arcs measure ~280 of a 2168 circumference —
                       an eighth of the circle each.

         .cut-circle   a second svg in #919191 holding the circle and four
                       crossing diameters, every one of them broken by a dash
                       pattern. That gapping is the "cut": the lines read as
                       spokes near the rim with the middle cut away, which is
                       what stops eight lines through a centre looking like a
                       pie chart.

         The path data is generated below rather than copied — an arc and a
         diameter are just trigonometry, and generating them means the geometry
         stays correct if the radius ever changes. -->
    <!-- The statement, arriving from both edges to flank the dial.

         Only the ENTRANCE is borrowed from the earlier reveal — the two halves
         fly in on perspective + rotateY from opposite sides. None of the pinned
         wipe that used to carry it comes with it: there is no pin here, no
         second act, and nothing for the halves to hand over to. They arrive,
         they settle either side of the dial, and that is the whole move.

         Reversed, though. That version was a DEPARTURE: it rested centred and
         scrolling threw it apart. This one starts thrown and resolves, because
         here the text is arriving to frame something rather than clearing out
         of the way. Same transform, run the other direction.

         They settle on the dial's own centre line, flush to it — left half set
         right-aligned, right half left-aligned — so the three read as one
         object rather than a circle with words parked near it. -->
    <div class="dial-say" aria-label="Four disciplines. One practice.">
      <p class="dial-say-h" data-say="l" aria-hidden="true">Four disciplines.</p>
      <p class="dial-say-h" data-say="r" aria-hidden="true">One practice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="dial-ring" data-dial-ring aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="dial-glass"></span>

      <!-- The ripple, running as a CONTINUOUS TRAIN rather than one event.

           Four rings share a single phase, each offset a quarter-cycle behind
           the last and each wrapping back to the centre when it reaches the rim.
           So the surface never stops moving: the first ripple leaves as the hash
           breaks the dial's edge, more follow it the whole way in, and the last
           one runs out after it has seated. One expanding circle reads as a
           pulse; a train reads as water. -->
      <span class="dial-wave" data-dial-wave><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>

      <svg class="dial-face" viewBox="0 0 700 700">
        <circle class="dial-outer" cx="350" cy="350" r="345" />
        <!-- ONE marker, not four segments.

             Four coloured edges and four engraved words were saying the same
             thing twice — the words already name the four disciplines, so the
             segments were only decorating a point already made. What survives is
             the single live one: the wedge and its edge, tracking the pointer.
             Everything a dial needs is a face and one thing moving on it. -->
        <g class="dial-seg is-selected" data-dial-seg="track">
          <path class="dial-wedge" d={wedge(MARKER.from, MARKER.to)} />
          <path class="dial-edge" d={arc(MARKER.from, MARKER.to)} />
        </g>

        <defs>
          {LABELS.map((l, i) => (
            <path id={`dialArc${i}`} fill="none"
                  d={textArc(l.at, WORD_SPAN, WORD_R, l.cw)} />
          ))}
        </defs>
        {LABELS.map((l, i) => (
          <g class="dial-word">
            <text><textPath href={`#dialArc${i}`} startOffset="50%">{l.t}</textPath></text>
          </g>
        ))}
      </svg>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
